clautolisp-dialect-portability-warnings — verify the dialect portability-warning mechanism is implemented, with tests
====================================================================================================================

See also:
  - autolisp-spec/documentation/autolisp-visual-lisp-specification-draft.org
    chapter 25, "** Normative Rules: Dialect Portability Warnings" — the
    spec section defining the model this issue verifies against.
  - issues/open/autolisp-spec-defun-rest-parameter.issue — the first
    concrete construct (`defun` `&rest`) that must emit such a warning.
  - clautolisp/autolisp-reader/source/dialect.lisp — named dialects
    (:strict 101, :autocad-2026 104, :bricscad-v26 120, :clautolisp 133)
    and the dialect descriptor where a warning/escalation knob would live.
  - clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp:2276 — the unbound-variable
    dialect-gated branch, as a model for where/how a dialect-gated
    diagnostic is dispatched at evaluation time.

Context
-------

The specification now defines *dialect portability warnings* (chapter 25):
advisory, run-time diagnostics printed when an executing program reaches a
construct unsupported by the host targeted by the active dialect. The
defining properties:

  - Run-time, evaluation-tied: a warning fires only when the incompatible
    construct is actually evaluated. Guarded/feature-tested code that is
    never reached produces no warning.
  - Once per occurrence: each distinct source occurrence warns at most
    once per run, on its first evaluation. A loop around an incompatible
    expression warns once, not once per iteration. A different occurrence
    (another defun, another call site) warns separately.
  - Advisory: evaluation is unchanged; under a dialect whose target host
    supports the construct natively, nothing is printed. Optional
    escalation to error is a separate knob (cf. :unbound-variable-mode
    :strict-error).
  - One knowledge base: the catalogue of "construct X unsupported by
    host/dialect Y" feeds both the run-time emitter and any static linter.

The user reports never having seen any such warning printed — so the first
job is to establish what, if anything, is currently implemented.

Scope of work
-------------

1. Audit current state. Determine whether ANY dialect portability warning
   is emitted today:
     - grep the reader/runtime for an existing warning emitter and a
       per-occurrence dedup table; identify whether the plumbing exists
       and is simply unused, or is absent.
     - confirm whether warnings are wired to the active dialect at all.
   Record findings so we know if this is "verify" or "build".

2. Define the warning infrastructure (if missing):
     - a portability knowledge base / catalogue mapping a construct
       (special form, function, or argument shape) -> the set of
       host/dialects that support it, with a human-readable message.
     - a once-per-occurrence dedup mechanism keyed by source occurrence
       (definition site or call site), reset per run/evaluation session,
       NOT keyed by construct class.
     - a dialect-gated emit point at evaluation time (mirror the
       unbound-variable dispatch at autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp:2276):
       warn iff the active dialect's target host lacks the construct.
     - an optional warning->error escalation knob on the dialect
       descriptor, off by default (precedent: :unbound-variable-mode).

3. First concrete consumer: `defun` `&rest` (see the defun-rest issue).
     - Native under :bricscad-v26 and :clautolisp -> no warning.
     - Under :strict and :autocad-2026 -> warn once at the definition's
       first evaluation, message naming the function, e.g.
       "DIALECT WARNING: DEFUN FOO with &REST only works in bricscad and
       clautolisp".

4. Enumerate the other constructs that should warn. Walk chapter 24
   ("BricsCAD Extensions") and the cross-vendor hazards already recorded
   (e.g. anything BricsCAD-only) and build the initial catalogue. List
   them in this issue as they are confirmed; each entry needs the
   supporting host set and a message.

5. Tests (the core deliverable). For `&rest` first, then each catalogued
   construct:
     - fires once on first evaluation under :strict and :autocad-2026;
     - silent under :bricscad-v26 and :clautolisp;
     - a second distinct occurrence warns independently;
     - an occurrence evaluated repeatedly (loop) warns exactly once;
     - a guarded/never-evaluated occurrence warns zero times;
     - with the escalation knob on, the warning becomes the corresponding
       error and (only then) changes program outcome.
   Capture the emitted text so the message format is pinned.

6. Make the warnings actually visible. Since the user has never seen one,
   verify the output stream/sink the warnings go to is the one a normal
   `alfe`/clautolisp run surfaces, and that no verbosity/quiet default is
   swallowing them. Document how a user enables/sees them.

Out of scope
------------

- The static-analysis tool itself. This issue covers the run-time
  emitter and the shared catalogue; a static linter is a separate
  consumer of the same catalogue and can be a follow-up.
- Authoring the full construct catalogue in one pass — start with
  `&rest`, add entries incrementally as cross-vendor hazards are
  confirmed elsewhere.

Definition of done
-------------------

- The run-time dialect portability-warning mechanism exists and matches
  chapter 25: evaluation-tied, once-per-occurrence, advisory, dialect-
  gated.
- `defun` `&rest` warns per the spec under :strict / :autocad-2026 and is
  silent under :bricscad-v26 / :clautolisp.
- Tests validate the firing/dedup/guarded/escalation behaviours and pin
  the message text.
- Warnings are demonstrably visible in a normal run; how to see them is
  documented.
- DEVELOP bumped in tools/clautolisp/source/version.lisp.


Progress (branch fix-spec-pages, clautolisp 1.6.5)
--------------------------------------------------
DONE — the run-time mechanism now matches ch.25 for the &rest consumer:
  - Once-per-occurrence dedup: per-run table on the runtime-session,
    keyed by source occurrence (lambda-list cons identity). Loop warns
    once; distinct defuns warn independently; guarded/never-evaluated
    defun never warns (emit is evaluation-tied). New slot
    runtime-session-portability-warnings-seen in
    autolisp-runtime/source/model.lisp; helper
    %portability-warning-occurrence-seen-p in api.lisp.
  - Escalation knob: new :portability-warning-mode dialect-descriptor
    slot (default :warn) in autolisp-reader/source/dialect.lisp, exported
    accessor autolisp-dialect-portability-warning-mode; :error signals a
    :non-portable-construct AutoLISP runtime error (mirrors
    :unbound-variable-mode :strict-error), off by default.
  - Visibility: warnings go to *error-output* (stderr) — surfaced by a
    normal clautolisp/alfe run; documented on the emitter and in
    RELEASE_NOTES.
  - Tests (autolisp-runtime/tests/evaluator-tests.lisp): loop-warns-once,
    distinct-occurrences, guarded-silent, escalation-errors,
    default-mode-no-error. autolisp-runtime suite 355/355; full
    make test-sbcl 5293 checks, 0 failures. DEVELOP bumped to 1.6.5.

REMAINING (issue stays OPEN):
  - [pjb] Canonical message format. The emitter keeps the existing
    "[lambda-list-extension] …" text (pinned by the pre-existing
    variadic-warning tests). Spec ch.25 shows a *representative*
    "DIALECT WARNING: DEFUN FOO with &REST only works in bricscad and
    clautolisp" form. Deciding which is canonical — and whether to
    include the function name — is a pjb call, since switching the format
    means rewriting the existing tests. Not changed here.
  - Shared knowledge base / catalogue (DoD 2 "one knowledge base", DoD
    4 "enumerate other constructs"): only &rest is wired, coded inline
    (dialect-name case in emit-lambda-list-extension-warning). Factoring
    out a construct -> supporting-host/dialect + message catalogue, and
    walking ch.24 (BricsCAD Extensions) to populate it, is deferred — the
    issue itself asks to add entries incrementally as hazards are
    confirmed. The static-analysis consumer remains explicitly out of
    scope.


Progress (2026-08-09, clautolisp 1.8.26) — DoD 2 and DoD 4 done
---------------------------------------------------------------

The second REMAINING item, the shared knowledge base, is implemented.
Only the pjb message-format call is left, so the issue moves to BLOCKED
rather than TODO: there is no implementable work outstanding.

ONE KNOWLEDGE BASE (DoD 2). New
clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/portability.lisp holds a
PORTABILITY-CONSTRUCT registry keyed by label. Each entry records:

  - KIND       :provided (clautolisp implements it, advisory warning)
               or :declined (clautolisp deliberately does not, so the
               diagnostic explains rather than warns);
  - TAG        the bracketed diagnostic tag;
  - NATIVE-IN  dialect names whose target host provides the construct;
  - SILENT-IN  dialects that stay quiet anyway (:lax, :clautolisp);
  - SUMMARY + EVIDENCE, so an entry can be audited without digging
    through issues.

Query surface, exported and tested: FIND-PORTABILITY-CONSTRUCT (by
label, case-insensitive — AutoLISP symbols are),
PORTABILITY-CONSTRUCT-SUPPORTED-P, PORTABILITY-CONSTRUCT-SILENT-P and
MAP-PORTABILITY-CONSTRUCTS. The file is deliberately dependency-free —
pure data plus lookup, loaded before api.lisp and knowing nothing about
evaluation — which is what lets the still-out-of-scope static linter
consume it without starting a runtime.

Both existing emitters now take their SUPPORT decision from the
catalogue instead of an inline `case'. Their message TEXT is untouched
(see the blocker below), so output is byte-identical — except for one
latent bug the migration exposed and fixed:

  * `let'' was silent under :bricscad-v26 but WARNED under
    :bricscad-v25, because the hand-written case listed only v26.
    BricsCAD V25 provides `let'' just as V26 does, so that was a false
    portability warning against the dialect's own host. Regression test
    added (let-warning-silent-under-bricscad-v25). This is exactly the
    class of drift a single table prevents.

FIRST DECLINED CONSUMERS. SETF and LET* are catalogued as :declined and
consumed by a new recognise-but-don't-define hook in
RESOLVE-UNBOUND-FUNCTION-OR-SIGNAL — see
bricscad-undocumented-clisms.issue, whose last implementable item this
closes.

DoD 4 (enumerate the other constructs) — scoped as the issue itself
directs. "Out of scope: authoring the full construct catalogue in one
pass ... add entries incrementally as cross-vendor hazards are
confirmed." So the backlog is enumerated and made reproducible rather
than transcribed:

  clautolisp/tools/portability-catalogue/extract-bricscad-only.sh

reports chapter 24 = 473 BricsCAD-only names, 655 implemented builtins,
and the intersection = *215 silent hazards*. The intersection is the
right target, not chapter 24 wholesale: a BricsCAD-only name clautolisp
does NOT implement already fails loudly with :undefined-function, so it
is not silent. The hazard is the name that WORKS here and on BricsCAD
and fails on AutoCAD — (acos x), (vle-nth0 l), (mod a b) — which code
written against clautolisp picks up with no signal that it has left
portable AutoLISP.

Those 215 are NOT wired as warnings in this pass, deliberately, on two
grounds: the issue puts one-pass authoring out of scope, and each entry
needs its supporting-host set confirmed before it can claim anything —
chapter 24 says which names BricsCAD has, not which ones AutoCAD lacks,
and several (MOD, FLOOR, REMOVE, POSITION) are common enough that a
wrong entry would be loud and wrong. Adding one is now a single
REGISTER-PORTABILITY-CONSTRUCT form.

Tests: 12 added (catalogue lookup/case-insensitivity, unknown-construct
silence, native/silent sets, bare-& native nowhere, enumeration, the
v25 regression, declined setf + let* explaining themselves while
staying undefined, the note under all five dialects, and an ordinary
undefined function getting NO note). Clean from-scratch rebuild: 6321
checks, 0 failures, no undefined functions.

Docs: user manual gains a "Dialect-portability diagnostics" section
answering DoD 6 directly — how to see them (stderr, nothing to enable;
if you have never seen one you were probably running --dialect
clautolisp or --lax, which are deliberately quiet), the tag table, the
escalation knob, and the declined constructs. RELEASE_NOTES updated.

RESOLVED 2026-08-14 (clautolisp 1.8.31) — pjb settled the format
================================================================

pjb's ruling, verbatim in substance:

  1. The unique TAG identifying the warning is what we keep — it is
     what lets a reference documentation carry more detail than a
     warning printed while the program runs.
  2. Report the source file, the line, and the name of the function
     concerned. We warn only once per occurrence, so we can afford to
     spend a little time finding them.
  3. The warning text is as clear and concise as it can be, ideally on
     a single line.
  4. The exact message is NOT formally specified anywhere.
  5. Open a REGISTER of these warnings, keyed by the [tag], listing the
     message (or template), the detail of the variations between
     dialects, and the URLs to the diverging reference material. It
     becomes an annex to the user manual.

So the ch.25 "DIALECT WARNING: …" wording was never the question: the
bracketed tag stays, and the prose is free. What was blocking was a
false constraint — the issue recorded that "the existing tests pin the
message", but they do not: every test matches the TAG (and, in two
cases, the token in backquotes). Nothing pinned the wording. That is
why this landed without rewriting a single existing assertion.

What shipped
------------

The line is now:

  [TAG] FILE:LINE in FUNCTION: one short line

for example

  [lambda-list-extension] drawing.lsp:12 in COMPUTE-AREA: DEFUN `&REST': rest parameter not portable to strict

- The register lives in autolisp-runtime/source/portability.lisp beside
  the construct catalogue: TAG, TITLE, KIND, MESSAGE (a format control),
  ARGUMENTS, EXAMPLE, DIALECTS, RATIONALE, REFERENCES. The four emitters
  format from it through %PORTABILITY-DIAGNOSTIC, and the escalation
  error now carries the same text, so a wording change lands in all
  three places at once.
- The location: the FUNCTION is always reported — read off the live
  evaluator stack's :USUBR frames, except for DEFUN, which passes the
  name it is defining (its body is not running yet, so no stack walk
  could supply it). FILE:LINE comes from the debugger's source map,
  which is only populated on a debug load; the diagnostic degrades to
  "in FUNCTION" rather than fabricating a position.
- The "use --dialect X to silence" advice is GONE from the runtime
  lines. It is identical for every occurrence and it is what made them
  three lines long; it lives in the annex now (pjb's points 1 and 3).

The register covers 18 tags, not 4
----------------------------------

pjb grepped the tree and produced a list of 18 bracketed tags. Comparing
it against the sources found errors on both sides, and that comparison
is the reason the register has a completeness TEST rather than a
convention:

- [clock-cache] is a FALSE POSITIVE — a defstruct in cador's
  sysvar-api.lisp, never emitted as a tag.
- [dcl-debug] is developer tracing of the DCL renderer selection, not a
  dialect diagnostic.
- [enc-CODE] is not a tag but the TEMPLATE in a docstring: the whole
  enc-* family is emitted by SIGNAL-ENCODING-DIAGNOSTIC, which builds
  the bracket from a code keyword. Those tags therefore appear NOWHERE
  as literals, and a grep cannot enumerate them.
- Consequently three real tags were missing from the list:
  enc-invalid-value, sec-untrusted-load, sec-untrusted-open.

So a literal grep can neither enumerate these nor tell a tag from a
struct name. UNREGISTERED-DIAGNOSTIC-CODES walks the closed code sets
(*ENC-DIAGNOSTIC-CODES*, *SECURELOAD-DIAGNOSTIC-CODES*) instead, and a
test fails if a code is added without a register entry.

The 18 registered tags fall into five KINDS, kept apart because they
share the bracketed shape but not their mechanism: :portability (4),
:extension-notice (2), :vendor-divergence (2), :encoding (8), :trust
(2). The code families have no single MESSAGE — each call site writes
its own text — so their entries carry an example instead, and say so.

The annex
---------

clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-dialect-warnings.org is GENERATED
from the register by clautolisp/tools/generate-dialect-warnings-annex.lisp
(`make -C clautolisp dialect-warnings-annex'). It is tracked, because it
ships in the manual and is built to PDF/Info by the documentation
target's wildcard. `make check-dialect-warnings-annex' regenerates into
a temp file and fails on any difference; the GitLab `documentation' job
runs it, so the annex cannot silently go stale. The check was verified
to FAIL on a deliberately corrupted annex, not merely to pass.

Verified
--------

SBCL and CCL suites green (exit 0, every suite Fail: 0) with six new
tests: register completeness against the construct catalogue and against
both closed code sets, entry completeness (including that each example
shows its own tag), the message coming from the register on the real
emission path, the single-line rule, DEFUN naming the function it
defines rather than its caller, and the annex rendering every entry.
The annex was built to PDF and Info. No existing assertion changed.
